<section class="sp85-enable"><div class="sp85-shell sp85-enable-grid"><div><div class="sp85-eyebrow">From Architecture to Customer Rollout</div><h2 class="sp85-title">A Partner Model Has to Work After the API Is Connected.</h2><p class="sp85-copy">A scalable partner model must work through discovery, deployment, support and the next customer—not just the first integration.</p></div><div><div class="sp85-enable-steps"><div class="sp85-enable-step"><strong>Discover</strong><span>Clarify customer environments, workflows, device needs and ownership boundaries.</span></div><div class="sp85-enable-step"><strong>Validate</strong><span>Confirm the integration model, application path, authentication and site requirements.</span></div><div class="sp85-enable-step"><strong>Integrate</strong><span>Use SDKs, APIs, TimeTrack or CirrusConnect based on the agreed architecture.</span></div><div class="sp85-enable-step"><strong>Deploy</strong><span>Coordinate hardware readiness, provisioning, installation and production rollout.</span></div><div class="sp85-enable-step"><strong>Operate</strong><span>Support device lifecycle, diagnostics, updates, replacements and evolving customer needs.</span></div></div><div class="sp85-eval-callout">When a customer requirement goes beyond the standard experience, ZKTeco WFM can evaluate the need for configuration, reusable product enhancement or customer-specific development.</div></div></div></section><section class="sp85-evaluate tw-bg-white tw-py-[72px] max-[680px]:tw-py-[56px]">
<div class="sp85-shell sp85-eval-grid tw-grid tw-grid-cols-[.8fr_1.2fr] tw-items-start tw-gap-[58px] max-[980px]:tw-grid-cols-1">
<div><div class="sp85-eyebrow">Evaluate the Partner Behind the Clock</div><h2 class="sp85-title">What Software Companies Should Ask Before They Standardize.</h2><p class="sp85-copy">Time-clock features matter. But a long-term partner decision should also test the operating model behind those features.</p><div class="sp85-eval-callout">Do not only ask, “Can we integrate?” Ask, <strong>“Can this partnership scale operationally, economically and technically as our customer base grows?”</strong></div></div>
<div class="sp85-eval-list">
<div class="sp85-eval-item"><b>01</b><div><strong>How many integration models can we choose from?</strong><span>Look for flexibility across SDK, prebuilt application, APIs and cloud/device-management approaches.</span></div></div>
<div class="sp85-eval-item"><b>02</b><div><strong>Who owns the difficult device operations after launch?</strong><span>Provisioning, connectivity, monitoring, updates, diagnostics, replacement and escalation should be explicit.</span></div></div>
<div class="sp85-eval-item"><b>03</b><div><strong>Who designs and manufactures the hardware?</strong><span>Understand control over engineering, components, quality, availability and product evolution—not only the brand on the enclosure.</span></div></div>
<div class="sp85-eval-item"><b>04</b><div><strong>What happens when components or device generations change?</strong><span>Ask about end-of-life planning, successor models, application compatibility and migration expectations.</span></div></div>
<div class="sp85-eval-item"><b>05</b><div><strong>Can the platform cover different customer environments?</strong><span>Rugged sites, larger-screen workflows, different credentials, connectivity constraints and mixed workforce needs should not force a new strategy each time.</span></div></div>
<div class="sp85-eval-item"><b>06</b><div><strong>Does the commercial model create partner leverage?</strong><span>Evaluate engineering effort, support burden, hardware economics, lifecycle risk and speed to market—not only unit price.</span></div></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
